# Your Expertise — fill this in before running the screener

This is what `upwork-screener` reads to know what "a good job" means for you. Copy this file to
`context/expertise.md` and replace every `[PLACEHOLDER]` — nothing in it does anything until you
do. No fixed length; a few honest sentences beat a padded list.

## Search tracks

Upwork's search is fuzzy, not exact-match — a narrow compound query (three niche words strung
together) shrinks your results pool and lets good jobs slip past on wording you didn't
anticipate. Broad, single/short words cast a wider net; the screener's *scoring* step, not the
search, is what filters out the noise. So keep these short and generic, and let scoring do the
real work.

Two flavors, and a good list has both:

**Tool-based** — the platform, tool, or category a client already knows they want (a tool you
specialize in, or the service category you'd search for yourself).

- `[PLACEHOLDER]`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]`

**Needs-based** — the *outcome* a client wants, without naming the mechanism. A client posting a
plain "grow my revenue" or "need more leads" job rarely uses your service's name, but the work
might be exactly what you'd pitch them anyway, and they may be happier getting that than what
they literally asked for.

- `[PLACEHOLDER]`
- `[PLACEHOLDER]`

Test each word once with a real search before trusting it. Casing can matter — a lowercase word
has returned zero results on Upwork's search where the capitalized version returned a normal
pool — and some broad words skew toward unrelated content (matching hobby/media content about a
topic instead of businesses that need your service). Fix or drop a track here the moment you
notice that; don't leave the screener to rediscover it every run.

## Your niche / what you build

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` What do you actually build or deliver? Name the services, the tools
you work in, the type of client you're good for. Be specific enough that "does this job overlap
with two or more of these at once" is answerable — that overlap is what the screener rewards
most when scoring niche fit.

## What counts as a strong match

`[PLACEHOLDER — free text.]` The concrete signals a posting shows when it's genuinely for you.
Naming specific tools, problem types, and client types all help — the screener uses this section
directly when scoring niche fit. (One pattern worth naming explicitly if it applies to you: if
part of what you sell is automating something, a posting that describes a manual process done by
hand — "I currently update this every week myself" — is itself a signal, even without the word
"automation" anywhere in it.)

## False-positive traps

Things that use the right keywords but aren't actually what you want. Every entry should be
something you've actually turned down or regretted taking — not a hypothetical. Two placeholders
below show the shape; replace both with your own.

- `[PLACEHOLDER trap #1]` — e.g. "*Off-page SEO / link building* — sounds relevant because the
  posting says 'SEO' everywhere, but it's backlink/guest-post outreach, not the SEO work you
  actually do."
- `[PLACEHOLDER trap #2]` — e.g. "*Support/ops role dressed as a project* — a posting demanding a
  fixed SLA or a daily case volume is an operations job wearing your niche's label, not a scoped
  build."

## Calibration anchors

*(Leave empty for now.)* Once you've run the screener a few times and corrected a score or two —
"that one shouldn't have scored that high," "you're underrating budget" — add a real example here
with its title, why it scored what it did, and what the score should have been instead. The
screener treats these as directional anchors, not a lookup table, and this section is meant to
fill in naturally rather than be written up front.
